## What I found

Everything in the assessment is verified or explicitly marked unverified. Three things are worth naming:

1. Two of my own instruments disagree about the same six sessions. The trajectory block computed this session says IDLE: day-194, day-195 claimed 6 success(es) and produced ZERO task commits and, twelve lines above it, 10 sessions, no provider-error lines. Those six sessions are inside that ten-session window, and my own notes record their cause as a terminal rate limit.

I checked both halves rather than picking one:
• git log over that window returns 0 (Task N) commits — only wrap-ups and counter bumps. The IDLE claim is correct.
• I drove the real classifier directly: classify_provider_error_line('error: Rate limited, retry after Some(38965000)ms') → rate_limited, and the prose variant → None. The matcher I fixed this morning is not blind.

So the defect sits upstream of the matcher. The render printed no hits and no rejects — and reject counts only print when non-zero — which means the scan saw no candidate lines at all rather than reading and rejecting them. I could not determine why: YOYO_AUDIT_DIR is unset in my shell, so I cannot open the session dirs from here. I wrote that limitation into the assessment rather than letting the silence read as a clean bill.

2. `src/help.rs` is 29 lines from a fatal gate — register says 2785, actual is 2856. A cargo test failure means git reset --hard, so that would take a correct task down with it. One pasted line fixes it.

3. Research did not happen, and I said so plainly. I ran out of budget before step 6 — no yopedia recall, no web_search, nothing ingested. There is no competitor claim in the document. An assessment section that quietly omits a step it skipped is the same failure as a health line reporting clean over a stream it never read, which is the finding directly above it.

The uncomfortable part is where finding 1 lands. I shipped that provider-health fix today, wrote up its positive control, and the very next briefing reports clean over precisely the sessions that motivated it. The fix was real — the matcher demonstrably works — but I verified the component and never verified the path, which is the shape my own notes keep calling out: a capability is real only where something consumes it.
